(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-nervous-system-sensory-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Nervous System: Intro and Sensory — Physiology Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What type of sensory information does the dorsal column-medial lemniscal system transmit, and what is its key structural feature?","choices":["Only crude touch and itch sensations, via small unmyelinated C fibers with very low spatial fidelity","Touch, vibration, position, and fine pressure, via large myelinated A-beta fibers with high spatial fidelity, decussating in the medulla","Pain and temperature, via slow unmyelinated C fibers with poor spatial fidelity, decussating in the spinal cord","Only pain and crude touch, via a three-neuron chain that decussates immediately at the level of entry"],"answer":1,"exp":"The dorsal column-medial lemniscal system contains large myelinated A-beta fibers (30-110 m/sec) that transmit touch, vibration, position, and fine pressure rapidly, with high spatial fidelity maintained throughout the tract, and decussates in the medulla oblongata. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How does the anterolateral system differ from the dorsal column-medial lemniscal system?","choices":["It uses smaller myelinated (A-delta) and unmyelinated (C) fibers for slower transmission, decussates in the spinal cord, has low spatial orientation, and transmits a broad spectrum including pain, temperature, crude touch, and itch","It uses large myelinated A-beta fibers for rapid transmission (30-110 m/sec), decussates in the medulla, maintains high spatial fidelity, and transmits only touch, vibration, and position sense","It only transmits fine touch and vibration, using large myelinated fibers that decussate late in the medulla, thereby preserving precise spatial localization throughout the entire pathway","It never decussates, remaining entirely ipsilateral throughout its course, and transmits proprioceptive information exclusively to the cerebellum via the spinocerebellar tracts"],"answer":0,"exp":"The anterolateral system uses smaller myelinated (A-delta) and unmyelinated (C) fibers for slower transmission (0.5-40 m/sec), decussates in the spinal cord, has a low degree of spatial orientation, and transmits a broad spectrum of modalities including pain, thermal sensations, crude touch/pressure, tickle, and itch. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What clinical uses do dermatomes have?","choices":["Only relevant to the motor system, used exclusively to localize corticospinal tract lesions in the brainstem","Localizing spinal cord lesions and explaining rash patterns from viruses like varicella zoster that hibernate in ganglia","No clinical significance beyond textbook anatomy diagrams used in first-year histology courses","Only used to diagnose peripheral vascular disease by mapping regions of poor arterial perfusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"A dermatome is the area of skin supplied by sensory neurons from a single spinal nerve ganglion; dermatomes are clinically useful for localizing cord lesions and explain why viruses like varicella zoster (hibernating in ganglia) cause rash in an associated dermatome (shingles). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is notable about the representation of different body parts in the somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus)?","choices":["The hands have the greatest area of cortical representation, followed by the lips and tongue, while the trunk has the least","The trunk has the greatest representation, followed by the thigh and lower leg, while the fingers have minimal representation","The lips have the greatest area of cortical representation, followed by the face and thumb, while the trunk and lower body have the least","All body parts have equal representation except the genitals, which show a small area of disproportionate cortical mapping"],"answer":2,"exp":"The somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus) shows unequal representation of the body: the lips have the greatest area of representation, followed by the face and thumb, while the trunk and lower body have the least — illustrated by the sensory homunculus. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What happens when the somatosensory association area (Brodmann areas 5 and 7) is lost?","choices":["Only motor deficits occur, specifically spastic paralysis of the contralateral limbs and face","No functional deficit occurs, since the primary somatosensory cortex fully compensates for the loss","Complete loss of all sensation on the contralateral side, including pain, temperature, and touch","Inability to recognize complex objects, and neglect of (or refusal to acknowledge) the contralateral body/world"],"answer":3,"exp":"Loss of the somatosensory association area (Brodmann areas 5 and 7 in the parietal lobe, which deciphers complex sensory associations) causes an inability to recognize complex objects and can cause neglect of, or even refusal to acknowledge ownership of, the contralateral body. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is lateral inhibition, and what does it improve?","choices":["The capacity of an excited neuron to reduce activity of neighboring neurons, improving contrast and two-point discrimination","The capacity of an excited neuron to increase activity of neighboring neurons, blurring contrast and reducing two-point discrimination","A mechanism restricted to motor pathways that recruits additional neurons to amplify a weak descending signal","The convergence of multiple presynaptic terminals onto one neuron, allowing spatial summation of subthreshold inputs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lateral inhibition is the capacity of an excited neuron to reduce the activity of neighboring neurons, improving the degree of contrast and thus improving two-point discrimination; it occurs at every synaptic level of the dorsal column system. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"How do fast pain and slow pain differ in timing and character?","choices":["Slow pain only occurs at the body surface and is never triggered by deep tissue damage in muscle, joint capsules, or visceral organs","Fast pain is felt after 1 second and has a dull, throbbing, aching quality; slow pain is felt within 0.1 second and has a sharp, stabbing quality","Fast and slow pain travel via the exact same nerve fiber type and spinal tract, differing only in the intensity of the triggering stimulus","Fast pain is felt within 0.1 second and is sharp (e.g., knife cut); slow pain is felt after 1 second or more and is throbbing/aching, associated with tissue destruction (bradykinin)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fast pain (body surface only) is felt within 0.1 seconds and is sharp (like a knife cut or needle poke); slow pain (surface and deep tissues) is felt after 1 second or more and is throbbing/aching, usually associated with tissue destruction and bradykinin release. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Do pain receptors adapt to a sustained stimulus, and what does pain intensity correlate with?","choices":["Pain receptors adapt slowly, over several minutes, reaching about half their initial response before stabilizing","Pain receptors do not adapt to the stimulus; intensity of pain correlates with the rate of tissue damage, not the total amount","Pain intensity correlates with total cumulative tissue damage rather than the rate at which damage occurs","Pain receptors adapt partially within seconds; pain intensity correlates with the total surface area of tissue affected"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pain receptors do not adapt to the stimulus (unlike most other receptors), and the intensity of pain correlates with the rate of tissue damage rather than the total amount of damage. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is the main chemical mediator of pain resulting from tissue damage (slow pain)?","choices":["Acetylcholine (with contributions from norepinephrine and epinephrine)","Dopamine (with contributions from serotonin and histamine)","Serotonin (with contributions from calcitonin gene-related peptide)","Bradykinin (with contributions from potassium and proteolytic enzymes)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bradykinin is the main cause of pain resulting from tissue damage (slow pain), with potassium and proteolytic enzymes also contributing. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which fiber type and tract carries fast, sharp pain, and which carries slow, burning pain?","choices":["Fast pain: type A-beta fibers via the dorsal column-medial lemniscal system; slow pain: type C fibers via the anterolateral system","Both fast and slow pain travel via type A-delta fibers through the neospinothalamic tract exclusively","Fast pain: type A-delta fibers via the neospinothalamic tract; slow pain: type C fibers via the paleospinothalamic tract","Fast pain: type C fibers via the paleospinothalamic tract; slow pain: type A-delta fibers via the neospinothalamic tract"],"answer":2,"exp":"Fast, sharp pain is carried by type A-delta fibers (6-30 m/sec) via the neospinothalamic tract; slow, burning pain is carried by type C fibers (0.5-2 m/sec) via the paleospinothalamic tract — explaining the double sensation of pain (sharp pain followed by slow pain). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What neurotransmitter is used by A-delta pain fiber nerve endings entering the neospinothalamic tract, and where do most of these fibers terminate?","choices":["Serotonin; most terminate diffusely in the hypothalamus, providing poor localization of the pain sensation","Substance P; most terminate diffusely in the reticular formation, providing only crude localization of pain","GABA; most terminate in the cerebellum, contributing to motor withdrawal rather than conscious pain perception","Glutamate; most terminate in the ventrobasal complex of the thalamus, allowing good localization of fast, sharp pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glutamate is the excitatory transmitter of A-delta pain fiber nerve endings; the second-order neuron crosses immediately and most fibers travel via the anterolateral columns to the ventrobasal complex of the thalamus, allowing fast, sharp pain to be well localized. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What neurotransmitter is used by type C pain fibers in the paleospinothalamic tract, and how well can slow pain be localized?","choices":["GABA; slow pain is well localized to a single point, since virtually all fibers terminate directly in the ventrobasal thalamus before reaching the cortex","Glutamate; slow pain is precisely localized to a single point, since most fibers terminate directly in the ventrobasal complex of the thalamus","Substance P; slow pain is poorly localized, often only to the affected limb or body part, since most fibers terminate diffusely in brainstem reticular nuclei rather than the thalamus","Dopamine; localization is identical to fast pain, since these fibers also terminate primarily in the ventrobasal thalamus rather than the reticular formation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Substance P is the excitatory transmitter of type C pain fiber nerve endings in the paleospinothalamic tract; only 10-25% of these fibers terminate in the thalamus (most terminate diffusely in reticular nuclei of the medulla, pons, and mesencephalon), resulting in poor localization of slow pain. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What over-the-counter treatment can deplete Substance P from local nerve endings to relieve pain?","choices":["Menthol-based creams for temporary cooling relief","Prescription-strength lidocaine patches","Capsaicin creams (made from chili peppers)","Topical ibuprofen or diclofenac gel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Over-the-counter creams containing capsaicin (made from chili peppers) can deplete Substance P from local nerve endings, relieving pain thought to be mediated by Substance P (e.g., lower back pain, arthritis, fibromyalgia). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What role do serotonin (from the Raphe nuclei) and enkephalin play in the brain's endogenous analgesia system?","choices":["Both substances act only on motor neurons in the anterior horn, enhancing alpha motor neuron excitability during spinal withdrawal reflexes to pain","Serotonin from the Raphe nuclei stimulates dorsal column interneurons that secrete enkephalin, an opioid peptide causing pre- and postsynaptic inhibition of incoming C and A-delta pain fibers","Enkephalin increases pain transmission by directly facilitating glutamate release from incoming C and A-delta pain fiber terminals in the dorsal horn","Serotonin has no role in pain modulation at all, acting instead exclusively on mood and sleep circuits originating from the Raphe nuclei"],"answer":1,"exp":"Serotonin released from the Raphe nuclei stimulates interneurons in the dorsal horn that secrete enkephalin, an opioid peptide that causes both presynaptic and postsynaptic inhibition of incoming type C and A-delta pain fibers where they synapse in the dorsal horn. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which endogenous opiate is found in the hypothalamus and pituitary, distinct from enkephalins and dynorphin (found in the brainstem and spinal cord)?","choices":["Substance P","Serotonin","Bradykinin","Beta-endorphin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Beta-endorphin is found in the hypothalamus and pituitary, while enkephalins and dynorphin are found in the brainstem and spinal cord — together these major endogenous opiates suppress pain during stress and emergencies. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"According to the gate theory of pain, how does stimulation of type A-beta tactile fibers reduce pain transmission?","choices":["By increasing bradykinin release at the site of stimulation, which paradoxically desensitizes local nociceptors to further painful input","Via a type of lateral inhibition of pain fibers by mechanosensory fibers, likely the mechanism behind massage and electrical stimulation of skin for pain relief","By directly destroying pain fiber terminals in the skin, a permanent effect exploited by capsaicin-based topical treatments","A-beta fibers only affect proprioception and vibration sense, with no measurable effect on pain perception or dorsal horn transmission"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gate theory of pain holds that stimulation of A-beta fibers from peripheral tactile receptors decreases pain signal transmission via a type of lateral inhibition of pain fibers by mechanosensory fibers — likely the mechanism behind massage, liniments, and electrical stimulation for pain relief. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Where is cardiac (heart) pain classically referred to?","choices":["The right lower quadrant of the abdomen","Above the umbilicus only","The lower back","The neck, left shoulder, and arm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Referred pain is localized to the dermatome of embryological origin; cardiac pain is classically referred to the neck, left shoulder, and arm, while stomach pain is referred above the umbilicus and colon pain below the umbilicus. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What is the proposed mechanism underlying referred pain from visceral organs?","choices":["Intermingling of second-order neurons in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord that receive input from both skin and viscera","Direct anastomoses connect visceral afferent nerve fibers to cutaneous nerve fibers at the level of the dorsal root ganglion","Visceral organs are innervated by the same cutaneous nerve trunks that supply the overlying dermatome directly","Referred pain results from sensitization of peripheral nociceptors within the visceral organ itself, independent of the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"Referred pain from visceral sources is thought to result from intermingling of second-order neurons in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, which receive convergent input from both skin and visceral afferents. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"At approximately what temperature do cold receptors show their peak response, and at what temperature do warm receptors peak?","choices":["Cold receptors peak at 25°C (responding 7-44°C); warm receptors peak at 44°C (responding 30-49°C)","Cold receptors peak at 15°C (responding 5-40°C); warm receptors peak at 40°C (responding 25-45°C)","Cold receptors peak at 10°C, while warm receptors respond maximally only above 50°C, near the pain threshold","Both peak at the same temperature range (30-35°C), overlapping almost completely near normal skin temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cold receptors respond across 7-44°C with peak response at 25°C, while warm receptors respond across 30-49°C with peak response at 44°C; the relative degree of stimulation of each receptor type determines the perceived temperature sensation. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Where is cold receptor density highest and lowest on the body?","choices":["Equal everywhere on the body","Highest on the trunk, lowest on the lips","Highest on the palms only","Highest on the lips, lowest on the trunk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cold receptor density is highest on the lips and lowest on the trunk; there are also many more cold receptors than warm receptors overall. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Why is the brain uniquely vulnerable to insulin overtreatment in diabetics, even though brain glucose uptake is not insulin-dependent?","choices":["Insulin is required for all brain glucose uptake just like other tissues, so hypoglycemia from overtreatment causes only mild, transient fatigue","The brain stores several hours' worth of glycogen reserves, so brief episodes of hypoglycemia rarely cause any noticeable neurological symptoms","The brain can switch entirely to fat and ketone metabolism within seconds of glucose delivery falling, making it largely resistant to hypoglycemia","The brain relies almost exclusively on glucose for energy, has only about a 2-minute glycogen reserve, and overtreatment causing hypoglycemia can lead to psychotic disturbances and coma"],"answer":3,"exp":"The brain uses glucose for energy almost exclusively and has only about a 2-minute glycogen reserve; because brain glucose delivery uses the GLUT1 transporter (not insulin-dependent GLUT4 like most tissues), overtreatment with insulin causing low blood glucose can rapidly cause psychotic disturbances and coma. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How does increased arterial CO2 cause cerebral vasodilation?","choices":["CO2 directly binds specific G-protein coupled receptors on vascular smooth muscle cells, triggering relaxation","CO2 has no direct effect on cerebral blood flow, since only changes in arterial oxygen tension regulate vessel tone","CO2 causes vasoconstriction rather than vasodilation by directly lowering local H+ ion concentration in brain tissue","CO2 diffuses into brain tissue, raising local H+ ion concentration, and H+ ions cause vasodilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Increased arterial CO2 diffuses into brain tissue, raising the local H+ ion concentration; the resulting rise in H+ causes cerebral vasodilation, increasing cerebral blood flow. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What is the proposed role of astrocytes in matching cerebral blood flow to neuronal activity?","choices":["Increased neuronal activity causes glutamate spillover, triggering an astrocytic calcium wave that leads to release of vasodilatory prostaglandins, dilating arterioles","Astrocytes only regulate CSF production and reabsorption at the choroid plexus, playing no role in matching flow to neuronal activity","Astrocytes have no role in blood flow regulation, which is instead governed entirely by direct neuronal innervation of arteriolar smooth muscle","Increased neuronal activity triggers astrocytic release of endothelin, directly constricting nearby arterioles and reducing local blood flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Increased neuronal activity leads to increased glutamate spillover at synapses, which triggers an astrocytic calcium wave; this wave leads to release of vasodilatory prostaglandins, causing arterioles to dilate and matching blood flow to neuronal activity. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Over what arterial pressure range is cerebral blood flow (CBF) autoregulated?","choices":["CBF is never autoregulated","150 to 300 mm Hg","60 mm Hg to 150 mm Hg","0 to 60 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cerebral blood flow is autoregulated over a pressure range of approximately 60 to 150 mm Hg; above 160 mm Hg, blood flow increases with pressure and vessels begin to stretch, risking damage and rupture (stroke). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Where is most cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) produced, and approximately how much is formed each day?","choices":["The choroid plexus in the lateral ventricles; about 500 mL/day","The choroid plexus in the fourth ventricle; about 1000 mL/day","The arachnoid granulations lining the dura; about 150 mL/day","The ependymal lining of the third ventricle; about 300 mL/day"],"answer":0,"exp":"CSF is mainly produced by the choroid plexus in the lateral ventricles, with about 500 mL formed each day (compared to a total CSF volume of only about 150 mL), and performs a cushioning function for the brain. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"How does CSF glucose concentration compare to plasma glucose concentration?","choices":["CSF has no glucose at all","CSF glucose is about 30% of plasma glucose","CSF glucose is identical to plasma glucose","CSF glucose is double that of plasma"],"answer":1,"exp":"CSF glucose concentration is about 30% of that of plasma; CSF also has slightly less Na+ than plasma, about 15% higher Cl-, 40% of the K+, and only 1-2% of the plasma protein concentration. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What structural feature makes brain capillaries unique compared to capillaries elsewhere in the body, forming the basis of the blood-brain barrier?","choices":["Brain capillaries have unusually thin endothelial cells with no basement membrane, allowing rapid passive diffusion of most plasma solutes","Brain capillaries are identical in structure to liver sinusoids, with large intercellular gaps that permit free passage of plasma proteins","Brain capillaries are a continuous type with tight junctions between endothelial cells (unique to brain capillaries), reinforced by a thick basement membrane and astrocytic end-feet","Brain capillaries are fenestrated, like those in the kidney glomerulus, allowing free passage of large molecules directly into brain tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"The blood-brain barrier is formed by brain endothelial cells with tight junctions between them (a feature unique to brain capillaries), reinforced by a thick basement membrane and astrocytic end-feet, forming a continuous type of capillary that restricts substances to passing through the endothelial cell membrane itself. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What can cross the blood-brain barrier by passive diffusion, and what requires selective transport?","choices":["Nothing crosses the blood-brain barrier without active transport, including water, gases, and lipid-soluble anesthetics","Only large plasma proteins like albumin cross passively, while gases and small lipid molecules require selective transporters","Water, gases (N2, O2, CO2), and small lipid molecules cross by passive diffusion; glucose and amino acids require selective transport","Neurotoxins like botulinum toxin cross freely via specific carrier-mediated transport shared with essential amino acids"],"answer":2,"exp":"The blood-brain barrier allows water, gases (N2, O2, CO2), and small lipid molecules to cross by passive diffusion, while glucose and amino acids crucial to neural function require selective transport; the barrier prevents entry of potential neurotoxins such as botulinum toxin. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Does removal of the somatic sensory areas of the cortex destroy the ability to perceive pain?","choices":["Removal of the somatosensory cortex has been studied extensively and consistently abolishes both fast and slow pain perception entirely","Yes, pain perception is entirely cortical, since lower brain areas like the thalamus and reticular formation cannot generate conscious sensation","Yes, but only for fast, sharp pain, while slow, aching pain remains fully perceivable through subcortical structures alone","No — pain impulses reaching lower brain areas can still cause conscious perception of pain, suggesting the cortex is more important for determining the quality of pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Removal of the somatic sensory areas of the cortex does not destroy the ability to perceive pain, since pain impulses reaching lower brain areas can still cause conscious perception — the cortex is likely more important for determining the quality/character of pain. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What is naloxone (Narcan), and how does it work?","choices":["An NSAID that inhibits prostaglandin synthesis, used for chronic inflammatory pain like arthritis and lower back pain","An opioid antagonist that binds opioid receptors and can reverse/block the effects of opioids like heroin, morphine, and fentanyl, used to counter overdose","An opioid agonist that binds opioid receptors and enhances/prolongs the effects of heroin, morphine, and fentanyl, worsening overdose","A synthetic analog of Substance P that binds neurokinin receptors and enhances pain transmission at the dorsal horn"],"answer":1,"exp":"Naloxone (Narcan) is an opioid antagonist that binds to opioid receptors and reverses/blocks the effects of opioids such as heroin, morphine, oxycodone, and fentanyl, making it effective for countering opiate overdose. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 79"}]};
